From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 16:49:54 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] zink: free the slabs a client has stopped using

A slab of small buffers is freed only when the allocator reclaims its
last entry, and the allocator reclaims entries only when a request of
the slab's size finds no free one, stopping after the second entry
that is still in use. A client that stops allocating therefore leaves
every slab it filled allocated: an Android guest that closed a WebGL
page kept 20 empty 2 MiB slabs, 40 MiB, for as long as it stayed idle.

The buffer cache's reaper now also reclaims the slab entries that have
been free for a second and can be reused, so a slab whose entries have
all been free that long is freed, its buffer goes to the cache, and the
cache's own expiry frees it. A client that frees small buffers and
takes them again each frame keeps its slabs. A host's request to
release cached memory reclaims every entry that can be reused rather
than the first few.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
